// EXPECT: [E132]
// A field invariant the compiler ignores is an unpaid assume. A field refinement against a
// LITERAL is enforced when the struct is built and usable as a fact afterwards; one that names
// another field parsed and then did nothing at all — `B(5, 10)` compiled clean with
// `pos usize <= cap` declared, and no proof could use it either. A reader has every reason to
// believe such an invariant holds.
//
// Relational field invariants are planned (the `in`-invariant work) and not built, so this is
// refused rather than ignored — the same rule the IR applies when it marks a function
// `incomplete` instead of passing it silently. Nothing in std/ or tests/ used the form.
type BumpLike {
    cap usize,
    pos usize <= cap
}

func main() i32 { return 0 }
